\section{Discussion and Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

Whether RLVR outperforms SFT for recovering pruned reasoning models depends on how the model was pruned.
Under diversity-preserving pruning at 20--25\% sparsity, the two paradigms produce near-identical accuracy (mean $|\Delta| {=} 0.54$\,pp; max $1.8$\,pp), yet the pruning criterion creates a 4.4$\times$ peak reward gap.
SFT warmup performs the bulk of accuracy recovery (${\sim}$60\,pp from post-prune); GRPO exposes structural capacity differences via reward trajectories but adds negligible accuracy.
Extended training (300 steps) confirms convergence: Standard's reward plateaus (0.054$\to$0.056, $f_0 > 0.5$), while CKA-only reward rises from 0.22 to 0.44 yet accuracy remains 64.37\%, suggesting structural ceilings rather than insufficient training.

\paragraph{Why does diversity preservation enable RL recovery?}
Importance-based pruning collapses the model onto its dominant reasoning mode; CKA-only preserves representationally distinct pathways that sustain exploration (2.89$\times$ mean gradient magnitude vs.\ Standard; \S\ref{sec:extended_analysis}).
The $\alpha$ dose-response inverted-U argues against a pure layer-distribution confound: increasing $\alpha$ shifts RASP toward CKA-only's early-layer pattern, yet recovery peaks non-monotonically at $\alpha{=}2.0$; Random (uniform layers) achieves 50.72\%, 9\,pp below Standard despite similar layer uniformity.

\paragraph{Practitioner guidelines.}
Use \rasp{} at $\alpha \approx 2.0$ (Figure~\ref{fig:dose_response}); this recommendation is based on single-seed evidence and multi-seed validation is needed before deployment.
Maintain per-layer floors ($\geq$40\%), stay below 25\% sparsity (\S\ref{sec:sparsity_cliff}), and monitor early $f_0$ as a pruning diagnostic.

\section{Discussion and Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

Whether RLVR outperforms SFT for recovering pruned reasoning models depends on how the model was pruned.
Under diversity-preserving pruning at 20--25\% sparsity, the two paradigms produce near-identical accuracy (mean $|\Delta| {=} 0.54$\,pp; max $1.8$\,pp), yet the pruning criterion creates a 4.4$\times$ peak reward gap.
SFT warmup performs the bulk of accuracy recovery (${\sim}$60\,pp from post-prune); GRPO exposes structural capacity differences via reward trajectories but adds negligible accuracy at this sparsity range.
Extended training (300 steps) confirms convergence: Standard's reward plateaus ($f_0 > 0.5$), while CKA-only reward rises yet accuracy remains unchanged, suggesting structural ceilings rather than insufficient training.
Importance-based pruning collapses the model onto its dominant reasoning mode, while CKA-only preserves distinct pathways that sustain RL exploration (\S\ref{sec:extended_analysis}); the inverted-U in the $\alpha$ dose-response argues against a pure layer-distribution confound.\footnote{If the advantage were purely from layer distribution, increasing $\alpha$ (which shifts toward CKA-only's layer pattern) should monotonically improve recovery; instead we observe a non-monotonic peak at $\alpha{=}2.0$.}
For practitioners: use \rasp{} at $\alpha \approx 2.0$ (single-seed evidence; multi-seed validation needed), maintain per-layer floors ($\geq$40\%), and stay below 25\% sparsity.
